traveling with the un to bamiyan  it should take just over four hours to  drive to this mountainous province  but the route is far too dangerous now  once seen as the safest place in  afghanistan  recent violence has seen the un  recording over 47  000 internally displaced people in  recent weeks  bamiyan without doubt has one of the  best hospitals in the country  funded by the argo khan  but the hospital has been overwhelmed by  people suffering  after walking for days through the  mountains  i left because the taliban had captured  more districts when we came here all the  people were on the run  they were fleeing my husband works for  the police and wasn't able to come with  us  i took all three of my children to get  some help but i received no help  these are among 300 000 people  displaced across afghanistan since may  the un's humanitarian coordinator  fears if there's no ceasefire things  could get much worse  my fear is unfortunately  is something which gives me trouble to  sleep  i think we will be looking at some of  the worst strategies  which we have seen in the recent history  we traveled in the un convoy out of town  towards the mountains  [Music]  to a school built by the un refugee  agency unhcr  it was constructed to educate children  returning from  outside afghanistan instead these kids  all recently fled their homes  inside the country news of our visit  quickly spread  they thought that the governor is coming  here these women desperate for help  after abandoning their villages  they told us that the taliban are coming  so we fled from our homes  what could we do we were in the baba  mountains and walked all the way  it was freezing there there was no food  so we were obliged to come here  there's a reason people here are so  fearful there's a long history of  persecution  of the hazara the largely sheer ethnic  group that make up most of bamiyan's  population  they remember the taliban's harsh rule  in the 1990s  when they blew up the giant buddha  statues that had stood for centuries in  these sandstone cliffs  the united nations can only provide food  and shelter  when people first flee but we met this  group of families who left their homes  three months ago  and are now struggling all living in two  small rooms  one of the problems is that the un's  humanitarian appeal  is desperately underfunded they've  received just 37 percent  of the 1.1 billion dollars they need  this year  what we've seen here in bamiyan is just  a snapshot  of what's going on in many places in  afghanistan  people on the move fleeing conflict  human misery  that the un is warning could soon turn  into humanitarian catastrophe james  bay's  al-jazeera bamiyan province afghanistan
 

